@startuml model-d-sequence-complete
skinparam dpi 140
skinparam roundCorner 8
skinparam noteFontSize 9
skinparam sequenceMessageFontSize 9
skinparam sequenceParticipantFontSize 10
skinparam defaultFontSize 9
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true

title EAP-DID Authentication Exchange (Detailed)\nFigure 3 -- Trust Model D: Issuer published to the VDR; Peer and Verifier identifiers unpublished

box "Peer System" #E8F5E9
    participant "EAP Peer\n(wpa_supplicant)" as peer
end box
box "Access Network" #E3F2FD
    participant "EAP Server\n(hostapd)" as server
end box
box "Relying Infrastructure" #FFF3E0
    participant "HTTP Proxy" as caddy_v
    participant "Verifier" as verifier
    participant "Key Service\n<i>non-normative</i>" as keysvc
end box
database "Verifiable Data\nRegistry" as bc

note over bc
  <b>VDR</b>. Holds the Issuer DID document and
  credential status lists. Read by the Verifier
  during validation. The peer performs no VDR
  access at any point in this exchange.
end note

== 1. Method Negotiation and Presentation Request ==

peer -> server : EAPOL-Start (EtherType 0x888E)
server -> peer : EAP-Request/Identity
peer -> server : EAP-Response/Identity
server -> peer : EAP-Request/DID, Flags S=1, V=1
peer -> server : EAP-Response/DID, Flags V=1

note over peer, server
  The Start exchange negotiates the method version before
  any state is established. A peer that does not implement
  version V terminates the exchange here.
end note

server -> server : challenge = base64url(random(24 octets)) [RFC4648]

note over server #FFE0E0
  <b>Freshness.</b> The challenge is the only element of the
  request that the peer cannot predict. Signing it demonstrates
  that the presentation was produced for this exchange.

  A fixed challenge value causes every presentation produced by
  every deployment to carry the same nonce and audience, so a
  presentation observed once remains acceptable indefinitely and
  at any Verifier. The challenge MUST therefore be generated per
  exchange from a cryptographically secure source.
end note

server -> caddy_v : POST /present-proof/presentations/invitation\n{schemaId, trustIssuers:[issuer DID],\n options:{challenge, domain}}
caddy_v -> verifier : (proxied)
verifier -> verifier : mint did:peer for this exchange;\nconstruct OOB invitation [DIDComm v2]
verifier --> caddy_v : 200 {thid, invitationUrl, myDid}
caddy_v --> server : 200 {thid, invitationUrl, myDid}

note over server, verifier #FFF3E0
  The Verifier mints a new did:peer per invitation. Key material
  extracted at enrolment is therefore stale by the time it would
  be used, and the recipient key MUST be obtained per exchange.
end note

server -> keysvc : GET /peer-did-key?did=<myDid>
keysvc -> verifier : key store read (key_id="agreement")
keysvc --> server : JWK [RFC7517]\n{kty:"OKP", crv:"X25519", d, x}

note over keysvc #FFCCCC
  <b>Non-normative.</b> This interface discloses private key
  material and has no place in a production deployment. It exists
  only because the EAP server and the Verifier are distinct
  processes here. It is removed when the EAP server performs VP
  validation locally, at which point the key does not leave it.
end note

== 2. Request Transfer (Downlink Fragmentation) ==

loop fragment_size = 1300 octets; approximately 2 fragments
    server -> peer : EAP-Request/DID\nFlags: L (first only), M, V=1
    peer -> peer : reassemble
    peer -> server : EAP-Response/DID (Flags octet only)
end

note over peer, server
  <b>EAP-DID Flags octet</b>

  "" 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 ""
  ""+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+""
  ""|L|M|S|K|R| Ver |""
  ""+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+""

  L  Length Included. The following 4 octets carry the total
     message length in network byte order.
  M  More Fragments.
  S  Start.
  K  Reserved for key confirmation. MUST be set to zero.
  R  Reserved. MUST be set to zero.
  Ver  Version. This document describes version 1.

  Each intermediate fragment is acknowledged with a payload
  consisting of the Flags octet alone. The sender MUST NOT emit
  the next fragment before the acknowledgement is received; a
  reflected or replayed response therefore cannot advance the
  transfer.

  While awaiting the Verifier, the EAP server emits keepalive
  requests carrying only the Flags octet. The retransmission
  timeout reported to the EAP state machine is 5 seconds.
end note

note over peer
  The reassembled invitation (approximately 2300 octets) conveys
  thid, the Verifier did:peer ("from"), challenge and domain. The
  peer extracts the X25519 public key from the key agreement
  segment ("E") of the did:peer:2 identifier.
end note

== 3. Presentation Construction (Peer, Offline) ==

note over peer #DDFFDD
  <b>3.1 Verifiable Presentation, JWT serialization</b> [RFC7519] [VC-DATA-MODEL]

  Protected header:
    {"kid":"<VC sub>#authKey", "typ":"JWT", "alg":"ES256K"}

  Claims:
    iss    <VC "sub">          identity the presentation is made under
    aud    <domain>            audience named by the request
    nonce  <challenge>         freshness value from the request
    jti    "urn:uuid:"||b64url(random(16 octets))
    iat    current time
    exp    iat + 300
    vp     { "@context":[...], "type":["VerifiablePresentation"],
             "verifiableCredential":[ <VC JWT> ] }

  Signature: ECDSA over secp256k1 with SHA-256 [RFC8812], encoded
  as R || S, 32 octets each. S is normalised to the lower half of
  the group order; without this the same signature admits two
  valid encodings.

  <b>The "iss" value is read from the "sub" claim of the credential</b>
  and never from configuration. The peer is therefore structurally
  incapable of producing a presentation that violates holder
  binding. nonce and aud bind the presentation to this exchange;
  jti and exp bound its reuse even where the Verifier keeps no
  replay state.

  <b>3.2 DIDComm encapsulation</b> [DIDComm v2]

    {"type":"https://didcomm.atalaprism.io/present-proof/3.0/presentation",
     "id":"<thid>", "thid":"<thid>",
     "from":"<peer did:peer>", "to":["<verifier did:peer>"],
     "attachments":[{"format":"jwt_vc",
                     "data":{"base64":"<b64url(VP JWT)>"}}]}
end note

note over peer #DDFFDD
  <b>3.3 JWE construction</b> [RFC7516]
  alg = "ECDH-1PU+A256KW" [ECDH-1PU]; enc = "A256CBC-HS512" [RFC7518]

  Content encryption precedes key agreement, because the content
  authentication tag is an input to the key derivation.

  1. CEK = random(64 octets)
       MAC_KEY = CEK[0..31]; ENC_KEY = CEK[32..63]   (RFC7518 5.2.2)
       A256CBC-HS512 consumes two keys; the CEK is 64 octets, not 32.

  2. (eph_sk, eph_pk) = X25519 keygen [RFC7748]

  3. Protected header:
       {"alg":"ECDH-1PU+A256KW", "enc":"A256CBC-HS512",
        "epk":{"kty":"OKP","crv":"X25519","x":b64url(eph_pk)},
        "skid":"<peer did:peer>#key-N",
        "apu":b64url(skid),
        "apv":b64url(SHA-256(recipient kids, sorted, "." joined))}

  4. Content encryption (RFC7518 5.2.2.1):
       AAD = ASCII(b64url(protected header))
       IV  = random(16 octets)
       CT  = AES-256-CBC(ENC_KEY, IV, PKCS#7(plaintext))
       AL  = uint64be(bitlength(AAD))
       T   = HMAC-SHA-512(MAC_KEY, AAD || IV || CT || AL)[0..31]

       AL is appended last. Any other placement produces a
       well-formed tag that no conforming recipient accepts.

  5. Key agreement (ECDH-1PU, Section 2.2 of [ECDH-1PU]):
       Ze = X25519(eph_sk,       recipient_pk)
       Zs = X25519(sender_sk,    recipient_pk)
       Z  = Ze || Zs

       The ephemeral secret is first. Transposition yields a
       different KEK and the recipient's unwrap fails.

  6. KEK = Concat KDF with SHA-256 (NIST SP 800-56A 5.8.1) over Z,
     OtherInfo as specified in Section 2.3 of [ECDH-1PU]:

       uint32be(counter) || Z
         || len32("ECDH-1PU+A256KW") || "ECDH-1PU+A256KW"
         || len32(apu) || apu
         || len32(apv) || apv
         || uint32be(256)                 SuppPubInfo, keydatalen
         || len32(T) || T                 SuppPrivInfo, the tag

  7. encrypted_key = AES-256 Key Wrap(KEK, CEK) [RFC3394]

  The JWE has a single recipient: the Verifier.

  <b>3.4 Compression</b>
  gzip [RFC1952] reduces approximately 9400 octets to 7000, which
  is about five fewer EAP round trips.
end note

note over peer #E0E8FF
  <b>Rationale: ECDH-1PU versus ECDH-ES</b>

  ECDH-ES (used on the enrolment path, Figure 4) derives the KEK
  from a single ephemeral-static agreement. The recipient obtains
  confidentiality but no assurance of origin: any party may encrypt
  to a published public key.

  ECDH-1PU additionally incorporates the sender's static key, so
  the KEK is derivable only by a party holding the private key
  identified by "skid". Two consequences follow:

  o  Sender authentication. The JWE demonstrates origin from the
     peer's X25519 key.
  o  Ciphertext binding. Including the content authentication tag
     in the KDF (step 6) binds the wrapped key to this ciphertext;
     it cannot be transplanted to another.
end note

== 4. Presentation Transfer (Uplink Fragmentation) ==

loop fragment_size = 1300 octets; approximately 6 fragments
    peer -> server : EAP-Response/DID\nFlags: L (first only), M, V=1
    server -> server : reassemble
    server -> peer : EAP-Request/DID (acknowledgement)
end
peer -> server : EAP-Response/DID (final fragment, M=0)

== 5. CEK Recovery and Relay ==

server -> server : gzip decompression

note over server #E3F2FD
  <b>The EAP server opens the JWE only far enough to recover the
  key.</b> It does not parse the presentation and does not decide
  its validity; that is the Verifier's function.

  1. skid = protected header "skid"
     sender_pk = key agreement segment of the did:peer in skid

     The sender public key is obtained from the message itself.
     No peer-specific configuration exists on the EAP server.

  2. Ze = X25519(recipient_sk, eph_pk)      recipient_sk from Section 1
     Zs = X25519(recipient_sk, sender_pk)
     Z  = Ze || Zs                          same ordering as the sender

  3. KEK = Concat KDF(Z, ..., T)            same OtherInfo
  4. CEK = AES-256 Key Unwrap(KEK, encrypted_key) [RFC3394]

  <b>Failure is detected, not silent.</b> [RFC3394] specifies a fixed
  initial value A6A6A6A6A6A6A6A6 that is verified on unwrap. A KEK
  differing in any bit causes rejection. A successful unwrap is
  therefore evidence that the recovered CEK is the value the peer
  generated, and not merely a well-formed key.
end note

server -> caddy_v : POST /didcomm\nContent-Type: application/didcomm-encrypted+json
caddy_v -> verifier : (proxied)
verifier -> verifier : JWE decryption using its own private key;\nextract VP JWT from attachment

verifier -> bc : resolve VC "iss";\nretrieve credential status list
bc --> verifier : DID document; revocation state

note over verifier, bc #FFCC99
  <b>Validation performed by the Verifier</b>

  |= Check|= Property established|
  | VP signature | signed by the key named in "kid" |
  | <b>Holder binding</b> | iss(VP) == sub(VC); the presenter is the subject |
  | <b>Validity period</b> | iat/exp within the acceptance window |
  | nonce, aud | responds to this request |
  | VC signature | issued by the asserted Issuer |
  | trustIssuers | that Issuer is accepted by the request |
  | credentialSchema | credential conforms to the requested schema |
  | credentialStatus | credential is not revoked |

  The VC "sub" is a did:prism long form and resolves from the
  identifier alone. Only "iss" requires VDR access.

  Holder binding and validity period checking are disabled by
  default in the Verifier implementation and MUST be enabled
  (PRESENTATION_VERIFY_HOLDER_BINDING, PRESENTATION_VERIFY_DATES).
end note

alt presentation valid
    verifier -> verifier : PresentationVerified
else presentation invalid
    verifier -> verifier : PresentationVerificationFailed
end
verifier --> caddy_v : 200 OK
caddy_v --> server : 200 OK

server -> caddy_v : GET /present-proof/presentations (poll on thid)
caddy_v --> server : terminal state

== 6. Key Derivation ==

note over peer, server #E8F5E9
  <b>MSK and EMSK</b> [RFC5247], derived by both parties from the
  same CEK using HKDF with SHA-256 [RFC5869]:

    PRK  = HMAC-SHA-256(salt = thid, IKM = CEK)          (Section 2.2)
    T(0) = ""
    T(n) = HMAC-SHA-256(PRK, T(n-1) || label || n)       (Section 2.3)
    OKM  = first 64 octets of T(1) || T(2) || ...

    MSK   label = "EAP-DID-MSK"
    EMSK  label = "EAP-DID-EMSK"

    Session-Id = 0x39 || SHA-256(thid)     (Method Type 57)

  Feeding T(n-1) into the next block is what distinguishes HKDF
  from independent PRF evaluations. Omitting it still yields a
  pseudorandom string but does not interoperate with any
  conforming implementation.

  thid is unique per exchange and serves as the salt, so repeated
  presentations of one credential yield unrelated keys.

  Peer:    holds the CEK from Section 3.3.
  Server:  recovered the CEK in Section 5.
end note

== 7. Completion ==

server -> peer : EAP-Success [RFC3748]

note over peer, server #F0F0FF
  <b>4-Way Handshake</b> [IEEE 802.11], on wireless media only. The
  wired test deployment reports "no EAPOL-Key frames required".

    PMK = MSK[0..31]
    PTK = PRF(PMK, "Pairwise key expansion",
              Min(AA,SPA) || Max(AA,SPA) ||
              Min(ANonce,SNonce) || Max(ANonce,SNonce))
    PTK = KCK(16) || KEK(16) || TK(32)

  The handshake is where agreement on the CEK is confirmed. Absent
  it, a successful authentication would leave the link unprotected.
end note

server -> peer : EAPOL-Key (message 1: ANonce)
peer -> server : EAPOL-Key (message 2: SNonce, MIC)
server -> peer : EAPOL-Key (message 3: GTK)
peer -> server : EAPOL-Key (message 4: acknowledgement)

note over peer, bc #DFFFD8
  Controlled port authorised. The Issuer was not contacted and has
  no record of the exchange.
end note

legend top
  == Security Considerations (Trust Model D) ==

  |= Property|= Status|
  | Confidentiality against third parties | provided by the JWE; no TLS tunnel required |
  | Holder proof of possession | ES256K over the subject key; holder binding enforced |
  | Replay resistance | per-exchange challenge, jti, bounded exp |
  | Issuer authenticity | resolved from the VDR |
  | Revocation | credential status list |
  | Subject privacy | no subject identifier published to the VDR |
  | <b>Verifier authentication</b> | <b>not provided</b> |
  | <b>Rogue authenticator resistance</b> | <b>not provided</b>; follows from the above |
  | Verifier institutional authority | not provided; requires Model G |
  | Selective disclosure, ZKP | not provided; the VC is disclosed in full |

  An adversarial Authenticator MAY substitute its own identifier in
  the invitation and thereby read the presentation. It cannot replay
  the presentation to another Verifier: nonce and aud bind it to the
  exchange and exp bounds its lifetime.

